  "name": "MUSK ANTIBODY - MUSCLE SPECIFIC KINASE Ab",
  "code": "2810",
  "cost": 0,
  "interpretation": "<p>False negative results can be seen in patient on immunosuppressant theraphy</p>\n<table style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width: 99.9307%;\" border=\"1\"><colgroup><col style=\"width: 50%;\"><col style=\"width: 50%;\"></colgroup>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Index value&nbsp;</td>\n<td>Remarks</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&lt; 1.0&nbsp;</td>\n<td>Negative</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>=&gt; 1&nbsp;</td>\n<td>Positive</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Comments</strong></p>\n<p>Myasthenia gravis (MG) is characterized by weakness and easy fatigability that is relieved by rest and Anticholinesterase drugs. The weakness results from an autoantibody mediated loss of functional acetylcholine receptors in the post-synaptic membrane of skeletal muscle. Antibodies to muscle specific kinase (MuSK) have been found to be present in 40% of Acetylcholine receptor (AChR) antibody negative patients with generalized MG. Musk antibodies are rarely present in AChR antibody positive patients or in patients with MG limited to ocular muscles.</p>\n<p><strong>Clinical Uses</strong></p>\n<p>&bull; Diagnosis of autoimmune muscle-specific kinase (MuSK) MG<br>&bull; Second-order test to aid in the diagnosis of autoimmune MG when first-line serologic test (AChR antibody ) is negative<br>&bull; To establish quantitative baseline value for MuSK antibodies that allows comparison with future levels if weakness is&nbsp;worsening</p>",
